  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1250 PM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nA cold front will pass southeast across the area this evening into \nearly Wednesday. Ahead of the front, the HREF surface-based CAPE \nreaches ~1000 J/kg across east-central Wisconsin, although \nsynoptic forcing and deep layer shear are relatively modest. \nIsolated to scattered storms may develop ahead of the front, but\nthe severe weather risk will be quite low. Additional showers\nmay persist north of the surface boundary overnight into\nWednesday.\n\nA shortwave trough will drag a boundary south towards the area on \nFriday with a 20-40% chance of storms. Over the weekend into early \nnext week, the local area will likely reside on the northern \nperiphery of a seasonably warm, moist and unstable environment. \nSubtle shortwave troughs embedded in the seasonally strong mid-level \nwesterly flow  will interact with the low-level boundary and \ninstability to produce rounds of showers and storms across the \nMidwest. However, predictability of storms at this range is low in \nthis type of pattern. Some weather risk could evolve given the \ndegree of flow aloft, but these trends will need to be evaluated in \ncoming days as confidence increases with the degree of instability, \ntiming of shortwaves, and boundary placement.  \n\nOtherwise, temperatures will trend seasonably warm late this week \ninto the weekend. Some smoke aloft from wildfires may lead to hazy \nskies at times, but HRRR smoke model suggests low near surface smoke \nconcentrations through Wednesday.",
